            {
                "entry_id": "381026ae-8c0c-3c1a-889f-438888f04362",
                "word": "sophistic",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "925beaa3-2e4e-33cc-b5df-1dfef6946535",
                        "entry_id": "381026ae-8c0c-3c1a-889f-438888f04362",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The sophists of antiquity, in general or of a specific period; their beliefs and method."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "historical"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5435d82b-f9d5-4fda-bbab-22ea57c029ea",
                                "sense_id": "925beaa3-2e4e-33cc-b5df-1dfef6946535",
                                "text": "But when we consider his wish to reconcile Philosophy and Rhetoric, it must be remembered that, if the new Sophistic differed in most of its essential features from the Sophistic of Plato’s contemporaries, it was Philosophy as conceived by Isocrates rather than Plato that Themistius had in mind."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "66119e43-b615-4308-a278-9c6582cfdfe6",
                                "sense_id": "925beaa3-2e4e-33cc-b5df-1dfef6946535",
                                "text": "Rather, to use Whitman’s phrase, Sophokles “thought through” a great deal more of the sophistic than their attacks on religion or their fascination with religion."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a881f774-9824-4b07-882a-e2c01e9d258a",
                                "sense_id": "925beaa3-2e4e-33cc-b5df-1dfef6946535",
                                "text": "The luminaries of the ‘first sophistic’—Philostratus cites Gorgias, Critias, and others—treated, we are told, abstract philosophical themes."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
